window.LESSONS["d7-savr"].chunks.push(
  {
    level: 4,
    title: "Senior reasoning: narrating SAVR step-by-step with the hazard at each step",
    html: `<p>The junior "excises the valve and sews in the prosthesis." The senior narrates a sequence in which <b>each step carries a specific, named hazard</b> and a technique that avoids it.</p>
<p><b>Exposure and decalcification.</b> After aortotomy (a hockey-stick or transverse incision safely above the sinotubular junction and the right coronary ostium), the calcified leaflets are excised and the annulus is decalcified <b>down to healthy tissue</b> — but no further. Gouge too deep anteriorly and you perforate into the <b>LVOT or the aortomitral curtain</b>; too aggressively at the base and you create an annular defect that will not hold a suture. Every fragment is irrigated and suctioned: a dislodged calcium chip is a stroke.</p>
<p><b>The conduction-axis suture zone.</b> The hazard zone is the <b>nadir of the non-coronary/right-coronary commissure</b>, where the membranous septum and the His bundle run just beneath the annulus. Sub-annular pledgeted mattress bites placed too deep <i>there</i> injure the conduction axis and produce complete heart block. The senior keeps bites <b>shallow and precisely at the annular plane</b> in that region while placing them deeper elsewhere.</p>
<p><b>Coronary-ostial protection.</b> Throughout seating and knot-tying, the <b>coronary ostia</b> (left main typically ~13–15 mm, right ~15–17 mm above the annulus) must stay clear of the prosthesis and its sewing ring — a low ostium under a bulky supra-annular valve or a mal-oriented commissural post can be occluded.</p>
<p><b>Sizing to prevent mismatch.</b> The obturator sizes the annulus; the surgeon then predicts the <b>indexed effective orifice area</b> the chosen prosthesis will deliver for this patient's BSA and avoids patient-prosthesis mismatch (moderate below ~0.85 cm²/m², severe below ~0.65) — most consequential in the small, active, or low-EF patient.</p>
<p><b>De-airing.</b> Before the cross-clamp comes off, the ritual is obsessive: fill the heart, vent the aortic root, rock the table, ventilate the lungs to drive air out of the pulmonary veins, and watch TEE for residual bubbles — because air down the <b>right coronary ostium</b> (anterior in the supine patient) causes inferior dysfunction and RV distension moments after separation.</p>`
  },
  {
    level: 5,
    title: "PGY-5 mastery: the small annulus and the valve larger than the root",
    html: `<p><b>Viva bar:</b> <i>"Fit 68-year-old, severe symptomatic AS, 21 mm annulus — how do you avoid patient-prosthesis mismatch, and if you open and the annulus is smaller than you sized for, what exactly do you do?"</i></p>
<p>You <b>predict mismatch before the incision</b> from the indexed EOA the prosthesis chart gives for this patient's BSA, not after. When the annulus measures smaller than the valve you planned, the senior owns a <b>decision tree</b>, defended against an examiner who says "just put in the smaller valve and move on":</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Accept the mismatch</b> — reasonable only in a small, sedentary, low-demand patient in whom a modest indexed-EOA reduction is unlikely to matter.</li>
  <li><b>Buy effective orifice with the prosthesis</b> — a <b>supra-annular</b> bioprosthesis or a modern externally-mounted-leaflet valve delivers a larger EOA for the same annulus.</li>
  <li><b>Enlarge the annulus</b> — a posterior <b>Nicks</b> (into the non-coronary sinus) or <b>Manouguian</b> (across the commissure between the left and non-coronary cusps onto the anterior mitral leaflet) enlargement upsizes the annulus, and the <b>Konno-type aortoventriculoplasty</b> is reserved for the truly diminutive root.</li>
</ul>
<p>The defensible answer in a 68-year-old <i>active</i> patient with a 19–21 mm annulus is to <b>avoid severe mismatch</b> — choose a supra-annular valve or perform a Nicks/Manouguian enlargement rather than accept a small prosthesis that behaves like residual stenosis for the rest of their life. That is the two-rung climb from "bigger is better" to a named, anatomy-selected plan.</p>`
  }
);

window.LESSONS["d7-mitral"].chunks.push(
  {
    level: 4,
    title: "Senior reasoning: systematic degenerative mitral repair, segment by segment",
    html: `<p>Mitral repair is not a single maneuver; it is a <b>systematic segmental analysis</b> that dictates a lesion-specific technique. The senior walks the valve — A1/A2/A3 and P1/P2/P3 — identifies the Carpentier mechanism (Type I normal motion, Type II excess motion/prolapse-flail, Type III restricted), and repairs to a durable result.</p>
<p><b>Lesion-specific technique.</b> An isolated <b>flail P2</b> is addressed by triangular resection or, increasingly, a <b>neochordal (Gore-Tex) repair</b> that preserves leaflet tissue; anterior-leaflet or commissural prolapse favors neochordae over resection. Every repair is completed with an <b>annuloplasty ring</b> — sized to the anterior leaflet and the intertrigonal distance — to remodel the annulus, correct annular dilation, and protect the repair from recurrent stress.</p>
<p><b>The circumflex at the lateral annulus.</b> Deep annuloplasty bites near <b>P1</b> and the lateral annulus threaten the <b>circumflex artery</b>, which runs within a few millimeters of the annulus there (closest in the left-dominant heart). A kinked or ligated circumflex declares as a new lateral wall-motion abnormality after separation. The senior keeps lateral bites shallow and, if new lateral hypokinesis appears, suspects the suture and is prepared to take it down.</p>
<p><b>Testing the repair.</b> Before closing, the ventricle is filled with saline under pressure to assess the coaptation line and confirm a symmetric, non-leaking closure with an adequate coaptation length; post-bypass <b>TEE</b> then confirms no residual MR, no gradient, and no systolic anterior motion.</p>`
  },
  {
    level: 5,
    title: "PGY-5 mastery: the valve conference and the post-repair SAM rescue",
    html: `<p><b>The valve conference.</b> Mastery is choosing correctly across patients who each force one hard call and defending each on <b>evidence + durability + patient values</b>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Young bicuspid AS with a dilated aorta</b> → concomitant aortic (root or ascending) replacement, not an isolated valve.</li>
  <li><b>Small annulus</b> → root enlargement vs accept mismatch, decided on activity and body size.</li>
  <li><b>Ischemic secondary MR</b> → repair durability is poor (high recurrence after ring annuloplasty); <b>replacement with a complete chordal-sparing prosthesis may be the honest choice</b>.</li>
  <li><b>Asymptomatic severe primary MR with repairable anatomy</b> → <b>early repair beats watchful waiting</b> at an experienced center, before the LVEF falls to ≤ 60% or the LV end-systolic diameter reaches ≥ 40 mm.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>The SAM rescue.</b> You wean from bypass after a mitral repair and TEE shows <b>systolic anterior motion</b> of the anterior leaflet into the LVOT with a dynamic gradient and a new posteriorly-directed MR jet — classically after repair of a Barlow valve with excess posterior-leaflet height and a small ventricle. The error is to reach for an inotrope. The <b>ordered rescue</b> is physiologic first: <b>volume-load the ventricle, raise afterload with a vasoconstrictor, stop inotropes, and slow the rate</b> — a fuller, less hypercontractile ventricle widens the outflow tract and abolishes the obstruction. If SAM persists, go <b>back on pump</b> and revise: a <b>sliding leaflet plasty</b> (reducing posterior-leaflet height) or a <b>larger ring</b> moves the coaptation point posteriorly, away from the outflow tract.</p>
<div class='kbox danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>DANGER</div><p>SAM after mitral repair is a mechanical/physiologic problem, not a contractility deficit. Adding an inotrope makes the hypercontractile ventricle obstruct <i>harder</i>. Fill, constrict, slow — then revise if it persists.</p></div>`
  }
);

/* ---------------- Rotation-readiness merge: what to know before a valve case (from guide front matter) ---------------- */

window.LESSONS["d7-savr"].chunks.push(
  {
    title: "Before you scrub for a valve case: the five things to know",
    html: `<p>As with a CABG case, the generic pre-case questions (Day 1) apply, and a valve operation adds a specific set of facts. The mechanism of dysfunction and the echo numbers are what drive the repair-versus-replace and mechanical-versus-bioprosthetic decisions you will watch being made.</p>
<ol>
  <li><b>What is the valve and what is the pathology?</b> Aortic stenosis, mitral regurgitation, and so on — and by what mechanism (calcific, degenerative/prolapse, functional, rheumatic)?</li>
  <li><b>What are the echo findings?</b> Valve area and gradient, regurgitation severity, and LV dimensions and function. These grade severity and define the operative urgency.</li>
  <li><b>What is the operative plan?</b> Repair versus replacement — and if replacement, mechanical versus bioprosthetic, weighed against the patient's age and anticoagulation candidacy?</li>
  <li><b>Is there concomitant coronary disease</b> that needs addressing at the same operation (concomitant CABG)?</li>
  <li><b>What is the anticipated arrest and bypass time?</b> A longer cross-clamp raises the myocardial-protection concern and shapes the cardioplegia strategy.</li>
</ol>
<div class="kbox pearl">
  <div class="kbox-tag">PEARL</div>
  <p>For a valve case, lead with the mechanism and the echo numbers: "severe symptomatic AS, valve area 0.7 cm², mean gradient 55 mmHg, preserved EF" tells the whole story and frames every decision that follows in the room.</p>
</div>`
  }
);
